      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "Psychometric testing of a translated, culturally adapted questionnaire."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "Cross-cultural adaptation of a German version of the Oswestry Disability Index (ODI) and evaluation of its measurement properties."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA",
          "content": "The ODI, one of the most popular questionnaires for chronic low back pain (LBP), has been valid, reliable, and responsive. Recently, a Swiss version of the ODI has been published, but there is no validated version for Germany to date."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The translated and adapted German version of the ODI (ODI-G) was validated in inpatients with chronic LBP during 3 weeks' medical rehabilitation care. The ODI-G was completed at admission, 1 day later, and at discharge. Comparison with both a generic and chronic LBP-specific measure (the SF-36 and Hannover Functional Ability Questionnaire) assessed criterion validity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "A very high level of test-retest-reliability was found (r = 0.91). Criterion validity showed high correlations between the ODI-G on 1 side, and the SF-36 and Hannover Functional Ability Questionnaire on the other. Standardized response means showed significant changes when health status improved (1.38) or deteriorated (1.35)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The ODI-G is valid, reliable, and responsive. It may be used to measure current state as well as changes in health status, and allows for cross-cultural comparisons. Further research comparing the 2 versions in German language seems to be necessary."
        }
      ]},
